/**
 * Colorado Practical Applications (32 hours) — module question bank.
 *
 * THE NEWEST OF THE SIX OUTLINES. The Commission approved a revised outline at its meeting of 7 August 2012,
 * teachable from 1 January 2013 and MANDATORY FOR ALL PROVIDERS FROM 1 JULY 2013. Its own note says why: the
 * update "covers additional specialty areas of practice, incorporates a basic understanding of competency,
 * and allows education providers to update market trends."
 *
 * Rule 2.1.A.2.f fixes the floor: "Practical Application: not less than 32 hours." The Commission's seven
 * topics carry its own hours: 3 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 + 16 + 3 = 32. HALF THE COURSE IS TOPIC VI, Offer to
 * Closing, with fourteen sub-topics lettered A through N.
 *
 * THE COMPETENCY FOOTNOTE, attached by the Commission to Topics II through V and carried into Modules 02
 * through 05: "Educators should make sure students understand HOW COMPETENCY IS GAINED in each specialty
 * e.g. through working with an experienced mentor or partner and/or taking educational courses to become
 * competent in the field or area SO NO HARM IS DONE TO A MEMBER OF THE PUBLIC." Those four modules are
 * COMPETENCY modules, not surveys, and Rule 6.2.B's four routes — DECLINE; obtain the experience, training
 * and knowledge; obtain the assistance of the employing broker, a supervisory broker, a competent broker or
 * LEGAL COUNSEL COMPETENT IN THE MATTER; or CO-LIST — are the answer in every one.
 *
 * TWO REQUIREMENTS THE COMMISSION PUTS ON THE PROVIDER, in this outline's own words:
 *   "Providers should address EACH APPLICABLE STATUTE, RULE AND POSITION STATEMENT during the course. COURSE
 *    PROVIDERS ARE RESPONSIBLE FOR ENSURING THAT THE STATUTES, RULES AND POSITION STATEMENTS USED DURING THE
 *    COURSE PRESENTATION ARE ACCURATE AND UP-TO-DATE."
 *   "A test is required to establish a participant's satisfactory completion of the statutory requirements
 *    for the Practical Applications Course. Courses taught online must meet the requirements set forth in
 *    the ONLINE COURSE APPROVAL CHECKLIST created by the Division of Real Estate."
 * Neither the position statements nor the Online Course Approval Checklist was obtained by this project.
 * The second is a gate for EVERY distance-delivered course in this build, not only this one.
 *
 * RULE 6.10 IS DELIVERED IN FULL in Module 02 and revisited in Module 07 — the name rules, the prohibited
 * team-name terms, the two "independently owned and operated" statements, each viewable page carrying the
 * firm's name, expired listings removed within THREE DAYS including a written request to syndicators, the
 * FIRST CLICK OF THE MOUSE rule, past sales data requiring a cited source and a disclaimer, and no
 * advertising of availability or price without the owner's or owner's broker's authority.
 *
 * MODULE 07 STATES NO MARKET FIGURE OF ANY KIND, and neither does any item in it. The Commission left that
 * content to schools' discretion — the phrase "Schools discretion" appears four times in Topic VII —
 * precisely because it dates, and a figure written into a course file is wrong the month after.
 *
 * Each module carries one applied item, five engagement items and five remediation items. The correct
 * answer is authored at index 0 and permuted deterministically per item id at delivery. The public delivery
 * path strips correctChoice and rationale, and a test proves it. The 80 percent engagement and 100 percent
 * applied thresholds are STATECOURSE STANDARDS, and the items are NOT the test the outline requires.
 *
 * WHAT WAS NOT READ, AND WHICH THEREFORE APPEARS IN NO ITEM: CP 41 and every Commission position statement;
 * the Online Course Approval Checklist; the insurance rules in Chapter 3 of 4 CCR 725-1, not read in detail;
 * every Commission-approved form; the Commission's Transaction File Checklist; §§ 12-10-217, 12-10-218,
 * 12-10-219, 12-10-222, 12-10-304 and 12-10-602; § 38-12-101 et seq. and Colorado's eviction and unlawful
 * detainer statutes; any Colorado mediation, arbitration or interpleader provision; Colorado's foreclosure
 * and public trustee, entity, trust, probate, water law, oil and gas, conservation easement, zoning, land
 * use and property tax statutes; every local ordinance; the Colorado Fair Housing Act; RESPA, TILA, TRID and
 * ECOA; the federal Fair Housing Act; the ADA; the UCC and U.C.C.C.; federal securities law; copyright law;
 * and the federal lead-based paint regulations. Every figure in every computational item is supplied inside
 * the item as a given.
 *
 * Course version 1.0 submission draft — NOT APPROVED. No Colorado qualifying education hours are available.
 */

